  "body": "When looking at a set of numbers, we can spot unusual values called outliers by using the middle spread of the data. First, we find the median, which splits our sorted numbers in half, and then we find the quartiles that mark the lower quarter and upper quarter of the values.\n\n[[visual:1]]\n\nThe interquartile range is the distance between the first and third quartiles, measuring the span of the middle half of our data. We multiply this range by $1.5$ to set boundary lines called fences far beyond the normal spread.\n\nAny data point that falls past these outer fences is flagged as an outlier because it lies much further from the center than expected. In our numbers, 40 sits far beyond the upper fence, so the plot marks it separately.",
